out.push(S('Stage 7R · Share'));
out.push(A('⚠ **Two counts and one test.** **Seven of ten paragraphs had a _therefore_ outside the joining sentence, and in five of the seven the sentence was a conclusion sitting in the observation section.** **Then the test: could your partner accept every observation and reject your conclusion?** **Three of ten documents did not allow it** — **and then Dr Qabbani\'s two reports, and what the other side\'s expert did with his observation section.**'));

// ===================== 19.6 =====================
out.push(pb());
out.push(h2('19.6  The statement that will be attacked'));
out.push(S('Stage 1 · Get ready'));
out.push(A('**a** Open. ⚠ **The five parts from memory: instruction, scope, observation, conclusion, limit.** **Six of ten gave the five and four left out the scope** — **and the four are the ones to watch, because every other fault in this unit can be repaired after the document goes out and that one cannot.** **Write the five on the board and leave them there for the whole lesson.**'));
out.push(A('**b** Right: **2** and **4**. ⚠ **2 names the parts in the paper\'s order and adds the two that follow — the joining sentence and what is not concluded.** **4 is the same order in plain English, which is worth noticing: the order is not a technical convention.** **1, 3 and 5 put the conclusion first, bury the scope in an annexe, and invert the whole thing** — **and the room should be asked, of each, which question would be answered too late.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 2 · Listen and read'));
out.push(A('**a** **B** — ⚠ **the order in which the document will be attacked**, so that each answer is already in place.'));
out.push(A('**b** **1** ⚠ **Nine hundred to thirteen hundred words, and the one at two thousand four hundred was the worst of five** — **not because it was long, but because at that length he had started explaining the method, and every question of the morning came from the method section.**   **2** ⚠ **It must be worth what it says to the party that commissioned it.** **A statement with a hole in it is worth nothing to them, because the hole is the first thing the other side finds** — **and the party is then holding a document that has been destroyed in public.**'));
out.push(NOTE('⚠ **The distinction between a stated method and an explained method is the single most useful thing in this cycle, and it has to be given as a word count.** _Measured with a gauge calibrated in June, certificate at annexe 4._ **Eleven words: a certificate invites nothing.** **Three paragraphs on why that gauge is the right instrument is an argument, and an argument invites a counter-argument.** **Have the room find the method section in the two longest statements in the class and strike it to one clause in front of everybody** — **it takes two minutes and it is the only lesson in the unit that shortens a document rather than lengthening it.** ⚠ **And give the room Mr Zahreddine\'s arithmetic: of his five statements the longest was the worst, and the reason was not length.** ⚠ **The sentence the room needs most in this cycle is the one about the commissioning party, and it must not be given as ethics.** **A statement with a hole in it is worth nothing to the party that paid for it.** **That is a commercial fact and it is why the method is not a species of honesty: it is what the document is for.** **The solicitor\'s own words in 19.7\'s audio script say the same thing from the other side** — _I would rather have a weaker statement that holds than a stronger one that goes_ — **and a room that has heard both will not need the ethical version.** ⚠ **Then the narrow category of what stays out: a finding you cannot support, a period you cannot establish, and an opinion about anybody\'s conduct** — **and the reason for the third is practical, because a witness who gives one opinion about conduct will be asked for ten and will refuse nine in front of everybody.**'));
out.push(S('Language box · Practise'));
out.push(A('**1** **The instruction** — _to examine the first-floor slab and report on compliance; instruction dated 28 April, at annexe 1._   **2** **The scope** — **three to six items, a reason of a named kind under each.**   **3** ⚠ **Observations** — **with times, instruments and whose reading each is.**   **4** **The joining sentence** — **every source named, absences included.**   **5** ⚠ **What you do not conclude** — **with a reason for each, which is 19.4 arriving inside the conclusions.**'));
out.push(S('Stage 3 · Practise (sample – Card A)'));
