out.push(SAMPLE(['Mr Zahreddine signs his fifth statement this week, and the five parts are not a style but the order in which a man with a highlighter goes through it: what were you asked, what did you not look at, what did you see, how did you get from that to this, and what is your finding a finding about. Five questions, in that order, every time. Mrs Khoury said so the document answers them in advance, and in advance and in his words, which is the only advantage a witness ever has, because the same answer given in the document is a paragraph and given in the room is an admission, and the words are identical. Mrs Oueidat asked how long a statement is, and between nine hundred and thirteen hundred words, and the fourth one was two thousand four hundred and was the worst of the five, not because it was long but because at that length he had started explaining the method, and the method section was where all four of the morning\'s questions came from. Mr Attar asked whether the method is a weakness, and Mrs Khoury said an explained method is a weakness and a stated method is not, and the distinction is exact: a certificate invites nothing and an argument invites a counter-argument. Dr Qabbani asked about the party who commissioned it, and Mrs Khoury said a statement with a hole in it is worth nothing to the party that paid for it, because the hole is the first thing the other side finds and the party is then holding a document that has been destroyed in public. Mrs Rizk asked whether they ever ask him to take something out, and twice, and both times the paragraph they wanted out was the one that made the favourable finding credible. Mr Tayara asked what he does, and he writes back in two paragraphs, and both times the paragraph stayed, and once the instructing party told him afterwards that they had asked in order to see what he would say. Mrs Tarabishi asked whether there is anything he will not put in, and Mrs Oueidat said speculation, and the category is narrow. Mrs Ajouz asked whether the order of the five parts is fixed, and it is fixed because the questions are fixed, and the one that moves is the scope: two of his five statements had it at the end and both were reproduced without it. And Mr Zahreddine said the thing he will take away is the eleven minutes of reading the whole statement aloud, flat, in the other side\'s voice, before he signs, which has found something in every statement so far, including once a sentence that two colleagues and a lawyer had read and signed off.']));
